# Every test a platform cfg keeps out of the build, inventoried: 1,108 rows, and 443 of them gate nothing

Date: 2026-10-03. Host: Linux. The scan is static, so every number below is reproducible on any
host; nothing here claims a runner executed anything.

Guard added: `scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py` (scanner, ledger writer, checker),
`scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv` (the ledger, 1,108 rows), `scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py`
(35 fixture tests). Wired into `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (`workflows-present` job) and into the
`gates=()` array of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`.

Row closed: `TODO.md`, "the `#[cfg(unix)]` gating added to get the Windows leg passing is itself
coverage debt, with no ledger at all".


== 1. The debt, in the shape it was earned

`#[ignore]` already has an inventory (`scripts/ci/ignored-tests.py` plus a baseline file): an
ignored test without a recorded reason fails the build. A test behind `#[cfg(unix)]` had no such
inventory, and it is skipped just as completely -- more so, because it is not even compiled, so no
runner reports it as skipped and it appears in no platform's pass count.

The 2026-10-03 Windows leg made this concrete. A family of persistent-shell assertions went red on
`windows-latest`; the change that turned that leg green put `#[cfg(unix)]` on eleven of them. The
suite went green without any behaviour being fixed, and afterwards nothing in the repository could
say which tests had been gated for that reason, or what stopped being executed there.

The TODO row that asked for this guard had counted 397 tests carrying `#[cfg(unix)]` or
`#[cfg(not(windows))]` with a grep. That count is too low in two ways and too high in one, and the
guard exists precisely so the number stops being a grep:

  * it only looked at two of the possible spellings; gates naming one OS, gates written `all(not(..))`,
    `cfg_attr(<platform>, ignore = ..)`, gates inherited from a `#[cfg(unix)] mod tests`, and
    file-level `#![cfg(..)]` were all invisible to it;
  * it counted nothing at all on the Windows or macOS side of the same tree;
  * and it counted a test as "gated" wherever the string appeared in the file, which is not the
    same as the test being gated.

The measured truth, from the guard, is below in section 3.


== 2. What the guard holds still

The platform set of a test is the intersection of every `cfg` that applies to it, not just the
attribute on the `fn`. Five refusal classes are checked, all of them by failing the build:

  * a gated test with no ledger row (new gating needs a reason before it can merge);
  * a ledger row whose site is gone, or whose platform set, non-platform `cfg`, or marker set
    changed underneath it (the row was edited out from under the test, or the test was rewritten
    under the row);
  * a row without a real reason (`MIN_REASON = 20` characters), or one still carrying the import
    marker once the `--max-unreviewed` budget is spent;
  * more platform-blind files than `--max-blind-windows` / `--max-blind-macos` allow;
  * more gated tests with no platform assumption in them than `--max-assumption-free` allows, with
    the offending tests named rather than counted.

A file is blind for a platform if it holds at least one platform-gated test and no test at all that
would run on that platform.

Row identity is `(file, function, runs_on, kind, extra_cfg, assumptions)`. The line column is
deliberately excluded from identity: it is a navigation hint regenerated by `--write-baseline`,
because otherwise every edit made above a gated test would demand a ledger rewrite whose diff says
nothing. Comparison is a multiset comparison, mirroring `scripts/ci/ignored-tests.py`, because the
same test name can legitimately appear twice in one file under two modules with identical gating.

`kind` separates two debts that look alike in a grep:

  * `cfg-gate` -- the test is not compiled on some platform;
  * `cfg-attr-ignore` -- `cfg_attr(<platform>, ignore = ..)`, so the test exists there and is
    skipped there, and it runs where the condition is false (the polarity is inverted relative to a
    gate);
  * `cfg-gate-and-ignore` -- both at once, i.e. a test that is nowhere executed.

`extra_cfg` records non-platform `cfg` (features). It does not narrow the platform set, but a test
gated on both a feature and a platform is hidden behind two independent switches and the ledger has
to be able to say so.


