The two numbers worth reading are the platform column and the blind-file column. 62 of 1,082 gated
tests would be compiled on Windows: the Windows leg runs 5.7 % of the coverage that the Linux leg
runs, and that ratio was not written down anywhere before this. 74 files hold gated tests and no
test at all that runs on Windows, meaning those files execute nothing there.

The ledger file carries 1,108 rows, which is 1,082 gates plus the 26 platform-conditional
`#[ignore]`s; `scan()` returns every test in the tree and `ledger_rows()` keeps the ones some
platform does not build:

    $ head -3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv

    kind    crate   file    line    function    runs_on extra_cfg   assumptions     reason
    cfg-gate        chaos-engine    crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs  3497    headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint       linux+macos+other-unix  -       file-mode;posix-path;shell;std-os-unix;unix-ext-trait  inherited 2026-10-03 (pre-existing gating, not reviewed row by row)
    cfg-gate        chaos-engine    crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs  3702    workspace_symlink_escape_is_rejected    linux+macos+other-unix  -       std-os-unix;symlink     inherited 2026-10-03 (pre-existing gating, not reviewed row by row)

Every row currently carries the import marker, and `--max-unreviewed 1108` pins that. The budget is
a ratchet, not a description: it can only be lowered, so importing 1,108 unreviewed rows is what
makes the 1,109th impossible to do silently.


== 4. The `assumptions` column, and the 443 rows it names

The question the TODO row asked was not "how many tests are gated" but "which of them are not about
the platform they are gated to". The column lists the POSIX (or Windows) things visible inside the
test body and in the name of the file holding it; `none` means none were found.

22 POSIX markers and 10 Windows markers, matched against the test body with comments blanked and
string literals kept, plus a `file-name` marker when a word of the path is a platform noun:

    POSIX    std-os-unix raw-fd unix-ext-trait unix-socket signal fork-daemon file-mode symlink
             tty uid-gid posix-path fs-root shell posix-crate mount-lock unix-env x11-wayland
             mac-service systemd-dbus unix-tool errno nested-cfg-unix
    Windows  std-os-windows winapi win-call drive-path unc-path exe-suffix registry
             windows-shell errno nested-cfg-windows
    File     POSIX_PATH_WORDS: unix posix flock pty termios ioctl signal x11 wayland dbus systemd
                       umask chmod chown symlink libc rustix nix darwin macos linux procfs sysfs
                       mount setuid uid gid sudo ulimit rlimit fifo tty shm xdg clipboard socket pipe daemon
             WINDOWS_PATH_WORDS: windows win win32 winapi registry powershell ntdll jobobject
                                  taskkill conhost wine drive

Markers were widened until they stopped being embarrassing. The first cut used 14 markers and left
580 rows named `none`, including tests that call `xclip`, `flock`, `from_raw_os_error` and
`x11`-typed code -- i.e. gates that are obviously justified, reported as candidates for removal.
That list would have been read once and then ignored, which is how this debt survived in the first
place. The current cut leaves 443.

Portable spellings are deliberately NOT markers. `child.kill()`, `start_kill()`, `std::fs::rename`,
`Command::new("git")` all appear in tests that genuinely need one platform, and a marker that fires
on them would make `none` mean nothing. So `none` is a claim about a test's own text, and it is
wrong in two known ways, which are recorded in the guard's docstring rather than hidden:

  * the assumption may sit inside a helper the test calls -- calls are not followed;
  * the assumption may sit inside the production code under test, which the scan never reads.

`none` therefore says "look at this gate", not "this test would pass elsewhere". The cap is held by
`--max-assumption-free 443` and the rows are printed on demand:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet --list-assumption-free | head -4

    chaos-engine    crates/codegen/chaos-engine/tests/remote_cli.rs:369   each_command_spends_one_credential_and_the_file_keeps_working   linux+macos+other-unix
    chaos-engine    crates/codegen/chaos-engine/tests/remote_cli.rs:410   a_client_that_asks_for_less_gets_less                           linux+macos+other-unix
    xai-crash-handler       crates/codegen/xai-crash-handler/tests/integration.rs:149       handler_does_not_interfere_with_tokio_runtime   linux+macos+other-unix
    xai-crash-handler       crates/codegen/xai-crash-handler/tests/integration.rs:311       clean_exit_does_not_produce_crash_report        linux+macos+other-unix

`assumptions` is part of the row's identity, so a test cannot gain a POSIX call and keep an old row
that claims it has none (mutation M5 below). The blind counts and the assumption-free count are
checked, not printed, so all four numbers can only move down.


== 5. Fixture suite

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py
    Ran 35 tests in 19.850s

    OK

The classes are `Scanner` (cfg parsing: gates, `all`/`any`/`not`, per-OS spellings, inherited
module and file-level gates, feature `cfg` landing in `extra_cfg`), `EvaluatorUnits`, `Ledger` (row
identity, staleness in each direction, duplicate rows, short reasons), `BlindFiles`, and
`PlatformAssumptions` (marker found; portable body stays `none`; the Windows direction; a marker
that appears only inside a comment does not count; the budget fails and names; `--list-assumption-free`
prints the names; and growing a POSIX assumption makes the row stale rather than silently accepted).

Each negative case has the matching positive case in the same fixture file, so a scanner that went
blind fails something rather than passing everything. Two cases are about the ledger's own shape: a
row whose reason is blanked, and the same row twice.

`test_a_moved_line_does_not_demand_a_ledger_rewrite` pins the deliberate exclusion of the line
column: an edit inserted above a gated test exits 0. `test_the_shipped_ledger_names_gates_with_nothing_to_gate`
re-derives the first 25 shipped `none` rows from a fresh `guard.scan(REPO)` so the shipped ledger
cannot keep a stale claim about a body that has since changed.


== 6. Non-vacuity on the live tree: 12 mutations, all caught

The fixtures prove the scanner; this proves the guard refuses on this repository against this
ledger. Each case edits a real file, runs the shipped command, restores the file, and proves the
restore with `cmp`. The two files touched are `crates/codegen/xai-grok-paths/src/lib.rs` and
`crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/oauth_config.rs`.

    M1  a new #[cfg(unix)] test in a file that already has gated tests
        FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test crates/codegen/xai-grok-paths/src/lib.rs:350
              mutation_probe_not_in_the_ledger runs on linux+macos+other-unix only

    M2  the gated test behind a row is deleted
        FAIL: stale baseline row crates/codegen/xai-grok-paths/src/lib.rs:371
              contains_path_does_not_normalize_the_stored_root; the test is gone

    M3  #[cfg(unix)] on a listed test rewritten to #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
        FAIL: stale baseline row ... lexical_normalize_resolves_dot_segments_without_filesystem_access;
              the test is now ... runs on linux only
        FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test ... runs on linux only

    M4  the #[cfg(unix)] on a listed test is removed, making the test portable
        FAIL: stale baseline row crates/codegen/xai-grok-paths/src/lib.rs:350
              test_abs_path_buf_contains_path; the test is gone

    M5  a row recorded as assumption-free gains a std::os::unix::fs::symlink call
        FAIL: stale baseline row ... the test is now ... assumptions=std-os-unix;symlink

    M6  a gated test with a portable body is added
        FAIL: 444 gated tests show no platform assumption in their body, over the budget of 443;
              these are candidates to un-gate: ...

    M7  an ungated test gains #[cfg_attr(windows, ignore = ...)]
        FAIL: unlisted platform-gated test ... test_abs_path_buf_new_relative_fails
              runs on linux+macos+other-unix only

    M8  the reason column of one shipped row is emptied (in a copy of the ledger)
        FAIL: crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:3497 headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint:
              reason is 0 chars, needs >= 20

    M9  one shipped row duplicated (in a copy of the ledger)
        FAIL: duplicate baseline row: crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs
              headless_process_adapter_reads_real_json_entrypoint [cfg-gate] runs on linux+macos+other-unix only
        FAIL: 1109 rows still carry the import marker, over the budget of 1108

    M10 a file with no gated tests at all gains a Unix-only test
        FAIL: 75 files have platform-gated tests and nothing that runs on Windows, over the budget of 74

    M11 the unreviewed budget tightened by one, tree untouched
        FAIL: 1108 rows still carry the import marker, over the budget of 1107

    M12 the assumption-free budget tightened by one, tree untouched
        FAIL: 443 gated tests show no platform assumption in their body, over the budget of 442

    12/12 refused the merge. Every case exited 1 and printed the message its class is responsible
    for; none of the twelve was caught only by an incidental budget.

M4 and M7 are the two that would be easy to get wrong in the other direction. Removing a gate pays
off debt, and the guard still refuses: the row has to be deleted by whoever paid it off, because a
row that quietly disappears is how 1,108 rows would eventually leave. `cfg_attr` gating is the
opposite polarity from a `cfg` gate, so M7 checks the runs-on set is the complement (`windows`
excluded) rather than the intersection.

Afterwards, byte-for-byte:

    $ cmp crates/codegen/xai-grok-paths/src/lib.rs <pristine copy>
    $ cmp crates/codegen/xai-grok-mcp/src/oauth_config.rs <pristine copy>
    $ git status --short
     M .github/workflows/ci.yml
     M scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    ?? scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py
    ?? scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv
    ?? scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py

Both files restored identical to the copies taken before the matrix, and the only remaining diff is
the gate's own wiring.


== 7. The blanker, because it cost 3 minutes of every CI run

Reading a `cfg` correctly requires ignoring comments. The first version blanked them character by
character in Python and the full-tree scan took long enough that the guard was the slowest step in
its job. The replacement jumps between interesting positions with one compiled pattern:

    NOISE = re.compile(r"/[/*]|r#*\"|['\"]")
    jump = NOISE.search(text, i)

Since a blanker that is subtly wrong silently changes every row, the equivalence was proven rather
than eyeballed. `scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py` exposes `blank_noise(raw, keep_strings=..)`;
the same file's per-character predecessor was run against it on 400 real `.rs` files from the tree,
in both modes, and then against a whole-tree scan:

    blanker equivalence: 800 file x mode comparisons, no difference
    full-tree scan: fast 8.5s, per-character 18.2s, 2.1x, identical rows: True (13011 rows)

13,011 is what `scan()` returns before filtering; `ledger_rows()` keeps the 1,108 that some
platform does not build. The comparison of the two scans is of every column of every row, so the
optimisation is the same function measured twice, not a new one.

The `keep_strings=True` pass is built lazily, only for rows that are actually platform-gated,
because the assumptions column is the only consumer that needs string contents.


== 8. Wiring

`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, immediately after the spawn-cwd portability pair:

    # Platform-gated tests: which tests some OS does not even build, and which of those gates
    # nothing platform-specific. The ledger refuses new gating without a reason and refuses the
    # blind-file and assumption-free counts going up.
    python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py
    python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet \
      --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv \
      --max-unreviewed 1108 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 \
      --max-assumption-free 443

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` carries the same two commands as one entry in its `gates=()` array, so
the local Docker entry and CI run the same guard with the same budgets. `check-guard-wiring.py`
enforces both directions of that mirror -- that a script in `scripts/ci/` is reachable, and that a
reachable one is either run by the Docker entry or recorded in `scripts/ci/docker-entry-ci-only.tsv`:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (50 files in scripts/ci/, 49 reachable, 45 run by
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)


== 9. What this does not claim

  * `cfg` expressions are read as written on the item. `cfg_attr` is understood only in the
    `cfg_attr(<platform>, ignore = "...")` shape; any other `cfg_attr` is invisible here.
  * `cfg` applied from inside a macro (`include!`, a custom test-attribute macro) is invisible.
  * a file pulled in by a platform-gated `mod` declaration does not inherit that gate, so a file
    compiled only on unix reads as ungated. The scan covers every `.rs` file except `target/`,
    `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/` and `test-results/`, including members outside `crates/`, so
    nothing cargo builds is graded by a narrower walk.
  * `cfg(not(unix))` counts as covering `other` as well as `windows`, because that is what it
    covers. Nothing here is evidence that a platform leg ran anything: the ledger records what the
    source excludes, which is a different fact from what a runner executed.
  * a `none` in `assumptions` is a claim about the test's own text, wrong in the two ways spelled
    out in section 4.
  * the 1,108 rows carry the import marker, so this first commit records the debt without pretending
    each gate was reviewed. Reviewing one means replacing its reason and lowering `--max-unreviewed`.
